        + begin with the premise of coinciding ends between (i) and (iii)
            : re `(coincid)ing ends` see `a.+surprising.+larger ${same}ence` @
              ../02.wrong_rhetoric/._/text.notes.brec
            !! bad execution of repair method
                : re `repair method` see `^*\+ go straight into the unadorned argument`
                - an argument begins with a base premise that need not be argued for
                - that of coinciding ends between (i) and (iii) is not a base premise
                    : re `that (of coinciding ends between \(i\) and \(iii\))`
                      see `^*\+ begin with the premise ${same}$`
                - rather it is an intermediate premise that must itself be argued for
            | directly with a leading claim of that (i, iii) coincidence
                | a coincidence of ends evidences a source of morals whose law stands in force
                |
                    | [on] clear nights | [on|with] every clear night
                    - a [shining] coincidence of ends|{end direction}
                    -
                        | evidences
                        | {testifies | attests | bears witness} to
                        | {stands | shines forth} to {attest | bear witness | testify} to
                    - a source of morals | a moral source
                    | whose|{and a} law [in fact] stands|runs|holds [in force|effect] [everywhere]
            | directly with a leading claim of that (i, iii) coincidence,
              reinforced indirectly via the related (ii, iii) coincidence
                : re  `directly with.+\(i, iii\) coincidence`  see `^*\| ${same}$`
                : re `indirectly via.+\(ii, iii\) coincidence` see `^*\| ${same}$`
                : see notepad:2026-9-17c,d
            | indirectly via the related (ii, iii) coincidence
                : pace `^*\+ go straight`
                + infer from coincidence (ii, iii) → that also (i) coincides
                    | leaping to the latter (from my closest approach) by claiming it
                        / e.g. as ‘a further coincident|coincider’
                        → leaping into the argument
                            = the goal here
                    | wondering what explains (ii, iii)
                        → seeking what else natural selection (ii) and the safehold (iii) have in common
                            / aside from their coincident ends
                            → end direction, of course
                                ∵ it inheres in (ii, iii), it being a coincidence of ends
                                → that also practical reason (i) coincides is strongly suggested
                                    : see notepad:2026-9-17a,b : ∵ the known mechanisms|processes
                                      of practical logic include not only natural selection,
                                      but also, and even better known, practical reason
                + infer from coincidence (i, iii) → moral law



